Hi all,

Thanks for pulling this analysis together. I agree that feature coverage is an important consideration and, if both models genuinely deliver equivalent detection performance, then selecting the model with broader coverage is a sensible approach.

One question I have is around the performance metrics used to conclude that the models are equivalent.

Given the intended use case, this is fundamentally a ranking problem rather than a general classification problem. Operations only reviews the highest-scoring customers, so our objective is to maximise SAR detection within a fixed review capacity rather than optimise performance across the full score distribution.

With that in mind, I'm not convinced that PRAUC alone is the most appropriate metric for model selection. Two models can have near-identical PRAUC while exhibiting materially different performance in the extreme right-hand tail of the distribution, which is precisely where the model will be operationalised.

Would it be possible to compare the models using business-aligned ranking metrics, for example:

- SAR capture (Recall) within the top N customers reviewed (or at the expected operational capacity).
- Precision@K (SAR conversion rate within the review population).
- Lift or cumulative gain in the highest score bands.

If these metrics demonstrate that detection is genuinely equivalent, then I would fully support using feature coverage as the deciding factor. However, if one model consistently identifies more SARs within the operational review population, I believe that should take precedence over broader feature coverage, as it is more closely aligned to the model's business objective.

It would be good to validate this before we finalise the decision.

